// Fixture: Model-A intended reorder — two independent suspending calls with print side effects.
//
// `fa` waits 100ms then prints "A". `fb` waits 50ms then prints "B".
// They have no data dependency and no shared `lend` resource — the auto-parallel pass
// groups them as a Parallel pair and interleaves their polls.
//
// Under default-parallel mode, `fb` (50ms) finishes first → output is "B\nA\ndone".
// Under --no-auto-parallel, source order is preserved → output is "A\nB\ndone".
//
// This deliberate output divergence IS the intended behavior of Model A
// (design/concurrency.md lines 53-61): "independent operations run concurrently;
// either may finish first". This fixture is NOT part of the byte-identical sweep —
// it asserts the TWO SPECIFIC outputs and demonstrates that `wait` (not the absence of
// parallelism) is the user's tool for ordering independent side effects.
//
// Expected default output:  B\nA\ndone
// Expected sequential output: A\nB\ndone
function fa() -> nothing {
  wait sleep(100)
  print(`A`)
}

function fb() -> nothing {
  wait sleep(50)
  print(`B`)
}

function entrypoint() -> nothing {
  fa()
  fb()
  print(`done`)
}
